# The invariants this plan holds that a grep can hold.
#
# One rule per block, blank line between blocks, four fields each:
#
#   id       the rule name, and also the directory under near-miss/ that holds
#            the file this rule must fire on
#   paths    a grep --include glob deciding which files the rule reads
#   pattern  an extended regular expression; a match anywhere in a scanned file
#            fails the lint
#   why      what failure the rule prevents, and the issue that argues for it
#
# Adding a rule is adding a block here and a file under near-miss/<id>/. The
# self test derives everything else from the id, so a rule without a near miss
# fails the run rather than passing quietly.
#
# This directory is not scanned. A rule file naturally contains the strings its
# rules refuse, and a lint that reads itself refuses itself.

id: no-query-from-the-request
paths: *.cs
pattern: \[From(Query|Route|Body|Form|Header)\][^,)]*\b([Ss]ql|[Qq]uery|[Ww]here|[Oo]rderBy|[Cc]olumns|[Ss]elect|[Gg]roupBy|[Hh]aving)\b
why: An endpoint that takes a query fragment, a sort column or a column list from the caller lets the caller decide what the database does. Every report endpoint here takes named values and nothing else. Issue #55.

id: no-sql-built-by-concatenation
paths: *.cs
pattern: \$?"[^"]*\b(SELECT|INSERT|UPDATE|DELETE|FROM|WHERE|ORDER BY|GROUP BY)\b[^"]*("[[:space:]]*\+|\{)
why: A statement assembled from strings is a statement whose shape depends on its input. Parameters are passed as parameters, always. Issue #55, and the store issues it is built on.

id: no-identifying-network-or-path-detail-in-the-store
paths: *.cs
pattern: (RemoteEndPoint|UserAgent|User-Agent|\b[Ii]tem\.Path\b)
why: A network address, a user agent and a library file path are the fields that turn playback statistics into a record of where somebody was and what their disk holds. They are refused at the source rather than dropped later. Issues #27 and #48.

id: no-name-in-a-log-message
paths: *.cs
pattern: "[^"]*\{[A-Za-z]*(UserName|Username|ItemName|ItemTitle|SeriesName|EpisodeName|AlbumName|ClientName|DeviceName)\}
why: A log is read by more people than the database is, it is copied into bug reports, and it outlives every retention setting this plan can enforce, so the messages carry identifiers and not names. The pattern refuses the placeholder rather than the call, because a message template in this tree sits on a line of its own and a rule reading the call would not see it. That makes the rule stricter than the policy it enforces, which allows a title at debug level, and it names the placeholders rather than every one ending in a name, because a generic type parameter inside a cref ends in one too and that was measured. The near miss is the closest prior art's own start-up block, which writes the item title, the client and the device out at information level, one line each. Issue #48.

id: no-test-that-drives-a-browser
paths: *
pattern: (Selenium|Playwright|WebDriver|ChromeDriver|GeckoDriver|puppeteer)
why: A browser driven by a test needs a display or a downloaded browser and a running server. The page work is kept in modules with no document access and unit tested directly, and the endpoints are tested in process. docs/headless-tests.md, issue #21.

id: no-test-that-needs-a-display
paths: *
pattern: ([Xx]vfb|DISPLAY=|xdotool|\bXvnc\b)
why: A runner has no screen, and a test that borrows a virtual one has moved the problem rather than removed it. docs/headless-tests.md, issue #21.

id: no-test-that-asks-for-elevation
paths: *
pattern: (\brunas\b|\bnetsh\b|\bsc\.exe\b|requireAdministrator|Start-Process[^\n]*-Verb)
why: A test that needs administrator changes the machine it runs on, and on the machine somebody is sitting at it raises a consent prompt in front of them. docs/headless-tests.md, issue #21.

id: no-test-that-touches-a-certificate-store
paths: *
pattern: (dev-certs|X509Store|StoreName\.(Root|My)|\bcertmgr\b|\bcertutil\b)
why: Trusting a certificate is a change to the machine that outlives the run, and it is the change a failing https test most invites. Endpoint tests run in process, over no socket at all. docs/headless-tests.md, issue #21.

id: no-outbound-http-client
paths: *.cs
pattern: (HttpClient|IHttpClientFactory|HttpMessageInvoker|SocketsHttpHandler|WebClient|HttpWebRequest|WebRequest\.Create)
why: This plugin reads the server it runs inside and talks to nothing outside it. Proving that by watching the network needs a network namespace and root, which the headless policy refuses, so the client that would make the call is refused instead. That is a stronger statement than one observed quiet run, because it holds for runs nobody watched. docs/headless-tests.md, issue #21.

id: no-ambient-clock
paths: *.cs
pattern: (DateTime|DateTimeOffset)\.(Now|UtcNow|Today)|TimeZoneInfo\.Local\b|TimeProvider\.System
why: This plugin is about durations and calendar days, and a file that reads the machine clock or the machine zone behaves differently on the runner than on the server, and differently in June than on the last day of December. Every moment arrives as an argument: capture reads the timestamp the server put on the event, and a day is read in a zone that is named at the call. The suite is scanned too, because a test that reads the clock is the same defect with a shorter fuse. Issue #23.

id: no-server-value-in-a-page-asset
paths: *.html
pattern: \{\{[^}]*\}\}|<%[^>]*%>|@(Model|ViewBag|ViewData|Html)\b|%%[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*%%
why: The endpoint that returns a plugin page's content carries no authorization attribute and the server configures no fallback policy, so every page asset here is handed to an unauthenticated caller. A marker left for a server, a template engine or a build step to fill in is how a name, a total or an identifier ends up in a file anybody can fetch, and it is put there by whoever writes the page rather than by whoever reviews the endpoint. Values arrive after the page loads, over an authorized request, or they do not arrive. The rule reads the tracked file; PageAssetTests reads what was built out of it and also refuses an identifier that is not the plugin's own. Neither half can tell a value somebody typed in by hand from ordinary page text. Issue #63.
